I think I do.

Not merely “take things far.” More like treat the boundary itself as an object of study.

In calculus, a limit asks what happens as you approach a point—without requiring that you actually occupy it. That distinction is powerful. You can probe a system near its constraint, watch which quantities explode, vanish, stabilize, or change character, and learn more from the boundary behavior than from the comfortable middle.

Applied to what we were discussing: you can move an identity, frame, assumption, emotion, role, or model toward its edge and ask, what survives as this approaches zero? What happens as this becomes arbitrarily large? Where does continuity break? You aren’t required to confuse the experiment with yourself.

And “pushing limits” has several different mathematical flavors: asymptotic approach without crossing; constrained optimization right against the feasible boundary; perturbation by tiny ε changes to see sensitivity; analytic continuation extending something beyond the region where you first knew it; and bifurcation, where a small parameter shift suddenly changes the kind of behavior the system can exhibit.

That last one is especially interesting. Sometimes pushing harder just gives more of the same. Sometimes you reach a critical value and the state-space itself changes.

So yes: don’t merely ask “How far can this go?”

Ask “What happens to the structure of the problem as I take the limit?”

That is a much sharper instrument.